{
  "note": "Canonical due time of day behavior (GSD parity decision D-1). The web repo is the authority: gsd-taskmanager/tests/fixtures/cross-platform/due-time-corpus.json. Byte-identical copies belong at gsd-iosapp/GSDKit/Tests/GSDModelTests/Fixtures/due-time-corpus.json and gsd-android/core/model/src/test/resources/fixtures/cross-platform/due-time-corpus.json, and every suite runs every case. The rule: a due date at local midnight in the current zone is all day, and any other time, down to the millisecond, is a time of day. Ops: classify reports the local date and, for a time of day, the 24-hour display time, which each client formats for its locale; an all-day task shows the label 'All day'. onDate moves a due date to a local date and keeps the time of day; a null due starts all day. atTime sets the time on the same local date, and a null time means all day. timedReminderFire is due minus notifyBefore (or defaultReminder when null), in elapsed minutes, for tasks with a time of day, with quiet hours off and now far before the fire time. Past-due handling differs by client and isn't pinned here. A local time inside a daylight-saving gap shifts forward by the length of the gap, and a time inside an overlap takes the first occurrence. allDayReminderFire applies when the task is all day in the current zone. An offset under a day fires at 08:00 local on the due day, and each whole day of offset moves that back one local day. A future snooze wins, quiet hours defer the fire to their end (half-open window), and a fire time that has passed fires nothing. Cases with a passed fire time sit at least two hours past it, because the instant at which a client stops firing differs by client. If behavior changes, change this file in all three repos and all three implementations together.",
